    "https://careers.universiteitleiden.nl/job/PhD-candidate%2C-Dutch-morphology-and-constructicography/16897-en_US": {
      "title": "PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography",
      "description": "PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography Job Details | Universiteit Leiden PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies Home Vacancies Job Description PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography Vacancy number: 16897 Job type: PhD positions Contract hours min: 38 Contract hours max: 40 Location: Leiden Applying is possible up to and including: 1 November 2026 Apply now Faculty of Humanities, Leiden University Centre for Linguistics PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography Starting date: January 2027 (a later starting date can be negotiated) Deadline : November 1, 2026 Description Leiden University Centre for Linguistics (LUCL) invites applications for a fully funded PhD position within the NWO project ‘The Morphicon: A new gateway to morphological knowledge’. The PhD in this project will work in a small team to build a morphological constructicon for Dutch and use it to investigate the division of labour between grammatical regularity and lexical idiosyncrasy. Constructicons are digital networks of grammatical constructions in a language. Such resources are being built for a variety of major languages, but not yet for Dutch. Moreover, existing constructicons all focus on syntactic constructions, neglecting morphology. The aim of this project is to develop the first morphological constructicon or “Morphicon” with Dutch as the target language. The resource will be developed by a team of three researchers—the PI, a Postdoc and the PhD—in collaboration with the Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal. The PhD will participate in the team effort, but will also have their own research project. The team will work together on transforming existing lexicographic and grammatical resources into a digital network. The network will consist of lexical constructions (simple and complex words) and grammatical constructions (word-formation patterns), linked to one another by the properties they share. An important part of the work will be to find, test and develop digital tools to assist in classification, annotation and network building. The specific research project of the PhD will target the interplay between grammatical regularity and lexical idiosyncrasy in morphological constructions. To this aim, the PhD will investigate a set of selected constructional families in greater depth to assess the proportion and nature of idiosyncrasy within each family. This PhD position is part of an NWO-funded Open Competition Large project involving PI dr. Jenny Audring (Leiden University) and external partner prof. dr. Sjef Barbiers (Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal). Tasks and responsibilities Extracting word-formation patterns from the existing descriptive literature and encoding them in systematic constructional schemas; Matching complex words to the appropriate schemas and subschemas and annotating them in such a way that the inventory of constructions can be transformed into a network; Assisting in finding, testing and evaluating digital tools, including LLMs, to aid classification, annotation and network building; Investigating Dutch morphological constructions quantitatively and qualitatively; Presenting (joint) research results at workshops and conferences; Publishing (joint) research results and writing a PhD-thesis within the period of appointment; (Assisting in) teaching activities at bachelor level ; Participating in (the organisation of) knowledge dissemination activities; Participating in meetings and training sessions of the project research group. Selection criteria A Master’s degree in linguistics, completed before the start date; Demonstrable interest in grammar, especially morphology, from a descriptive and/or theoretical perspective; Native or near-native proficiency of Dutch and a good command of academic English; Excellent research and analytical skills; Experience in computational linguistics and/or affinity with the framework of Construction Grammar is a plus. Terms and conditions Appointment will be according to the terms of the Collective Labour Agreement of Dutch Universities (CAO Nederlandse Universiteiten). The employment will be for a period of four years. The first contract will be for 12 months with an extension for the following 36 months contingent on performance and compatibility. You are expected to work in Leiden and t he appointment must lead to the completion of a PhD thesis. Salary range from € 3.204.- gross per month in the first year for a fulltime appointment to € 4,051.- in the fourth year (pay scale for PhDs, in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities), plus substantial additional holiday and year-end allowances. Candidates from outside the Netherlands may be eligible for a substantial tax break. Starting date: January 2027; a later starting date can be negotiated. Research at our faculty/institute Leiden University Centre for Linguistics ( LUCL ) is one of seven Institutes that falls under the Faculty of Humanities. With over 120 linguists working at the Institute, it is a rich research environment that prides itself on fostering scientific excellence. At LUCL we offer extensive research support to our researchers. LUCL has a longstanding tradition in the study of the world’s languages and features unique linguistic expertise. Current theoretical insights are combined with modern experimental methods in its research profile area ‘Language Diversity in the World’. Researchers from our six umbrella research groups are experts in their respective fields while finding innovative ways of collaborating with colleagues in other fields and disciplines. The Faculty of Humanities at Leiden University is a unique international centre for the advanced study of languages, cultures, arts, and societies worldwide, in their historical contexts from prehistory to the present.  Our faculty is home to more than 6,000 students and 800 staff members. For more information see: https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/humanities. Diversity Fostering an inclusive community is a central element of the values and vision of Leiden University. Leiden University is committed to becoming an inclusive community which enables all students and staff to feel valued and respected and to develop their full potential. Diversity in experiences and perspectives enriches our teaching and strengthens our research. High quality teaching and research is inclusive. Questions? For further information on the research project and the PhD position please contact the PI, dr. Jenny Audring, j.audring@hum.leidenuniv.nl For practical questions, please contact the Institute Manager, Maarit van Gammeren: m.s.k.van.gammeren@hum.leidenuniv.nl . Applications Applications should be submitted no later than 1 November, 2026 via the blue button in our application system. Your application should include the following information: A cover letter, in which you explain your motivation; A full academic CV; A list of all Bachelor and Master-level modules you have taken, with an official transcript of grades; A writing sample, preferably your MA thesis and an article or term paper that you wrote that pertains to the topic of the position; The names and email addresses of two referees, including your MA thesis supervisor(s), who may be approached by the selection committee (no reference letters). The vacancy closes on 1 November, 2026. Interviews will take place in the second half of November 2026. Enquiries from agencies are not appreciated. Apply now Information at a Glance Create Job Alert Faculty / Unit: Faculty of Humanities Contact: Dr. Jenny Audring Contact Email address: j.audring@hum.leidenuniv.nl Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Study programmes Bachelor's programmes Master's programmes PhD programmes Education for Professionals Summer Schools Other modes of study Bachelor's Open Days Master's Open Days Admission & Application Organisation Archaeology Governance and Global Affairs Humanities Law Medicine/LUMC Science Social and Behavioural Sciences African Studies Centre Leiden Honours Academy ICLON (Graduate School of Teaching) About us Working at Leiden University Support Leiden University Alumni Impact Leiden-Delft-Erasmus Universities Locations Disclaimer Cookies Privacy Contact × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data center \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary)  prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimizing video performance, or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like, and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies ​ ​"
