    "https://careers.universiteitleiden.nl/job/Professor-of-Social-Decision-Making/16849-en_US": {
      "title": "Professor of Social Decision-Making",
      "description": "Professor of Social Decision-Making Job Details | Universiteit Leiden We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies Home Vacancies Job Description Professor of Social Decision-Making Job requisition Id: 16849 Job type: Academic staff Contract hours min: 38 Contract hours max: 38 Location: Leiden Applying is possible until: 23 October 2026 Apply now Professor of Social Decision-Making Institute of Psychology, Leiden University Are you a leading researcher in social decision-making, ready to help shape the future of a thriving unit? The Social, Economic, and Organisational Psychology (SEO) unit is looking for a professor to help drive its research, teaching, and societal impact forward. What you will do The research within this chair focuses on the fundamental mechanisms underlying social decision-making, with particular attention to interpersonal and/or intergroup processes – such as cooperation and conflict, trust and distrust, justice and fairness, social and economic inequality, power and status, stress and resilience, and group dynamics and social influence. The chair complements the two existing chairs within SEO: Social Cognition and Decision Making ( Lotte van Dillen ) and Economic Psychology and its Applications ( Wilco van Dijk ). You will play a central role in three areas: research, teaching, and leadership. You lead a research programme of high scientific quality and broad societal relevance that fits this profile, and take a leading role in the strategic development of research lines of the SEO unit. You actively secure external funding, supervise PhD candidates, and support colleagues in strengthening their research skills. You develop and deliver high-quality teaching within the bachelor's, master's, and/or research master's programmes in Psychology, and contribute to the strategic development of existing and new teaching programmes. You use a connective leadership style that brings people, ideas, and initiatives together, and actively contribute to a healthy, stimulating work climate where colleagues have room to develop and grow. You actively advocate the principles of Open Science and Recognition & Rewards (see the Leiden Academia in Motion programme), and help strengthen a work culture centred on Team Science and interdisciplinary collaboration. You take on governance and leadership roles within the section, institute, faculty, and/or university. Where you will work The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences consists of five institutes: the Centre for Science and Technology Studies, Cultural Anthropology and Development Sociology, Education and Child Studies, Political Science, and Psychology. The faculty is home to approximately 7,000 students and 1,000 staff members. Our institutes are dedicated not only to education but also to groundbreaking research that expands our understanding of human behavior and societal structures. What makes our faculty unique is the diversity of research topics, the variety of teaching approaches, and the structure of professional support. This provides you with the opportunity to explore and develop your interests and expertise. For an introduction to our faculty, visit our website: Welcome to the Leiden Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences – Leiden University . The Institute of Psychology trains new generations of students to address the challenges of our society, both now and in the future, through leading behavioral science research and education. This is achieved through collaboration, both within and outside the institute, in the field of psychology and related research areas, covering both fundamental and applied aspects. The broad bachelor’s program and a wide range of master’s specializations further contribute to this mission. Within the institute and the academic community, a dedicated and passionate atmosphere prevails. The themes of Health and Wellbeing, Social-Cognitive-Affective Decision Making, Development and Learning, and Advanced Behavioral Science Methods guide both research and education. The institute places great importance on fostering a safe and inclusive environment, with room for new developments such as open science, recognition and valorization, and interdisciplinarity, which are firmly embedded in the institute’s strategy. The institute, with approximately 5,000 students and 600 staff members, comprises the Institute Office, the Bachelor’s Education Department, and six research and teaching units: Health, Medical and Neuropsychology, Clinical Psychology, Cognitive Psychology, Developmental and Educational Psychology, Social and Organizational Psychology, and Methods and Statistics. The Social, Economic, and Organisational Psychology (SEO) unit The SEO unit conducts scientific research, provides academic education, and actively applies knowledge for the benefit of society. Human behaviour is studied within its social context – and these insights are used to promote choices that strengthen the wellbeing of individuals, groups, and society as a whole. Our research, teaching, and societal impact are not separate tasks but form a coherent whole. Research expands fundamental knowledge of human behaviour. Teaching trains students to become skilled, critically thinking academics and science-based professionals. And insights gained are actively translated into policy advice, professional training, and collaboration with societal partners – with knowledge exchange explicitly working in both directions: knowledge is shared with society, and questions and insights are actively gathered from practice. We strive for research that is independent, of high integrity, and transparent – meeting the highest standards of ethics, reproducibility, and robustness, and in line with the principles of Open Science. Societal impact is not a side note here, but an integral part of our work as a unit. The SEO unit is well embedded within Leiden University: both within the Institute of Psychology and in connection with disciplines such as Public Administration, Economics, and Law. Nationally, societal partners – such as the ministry of Finance, the ministry of Education, Culture and Science (OCW), and the ministry of Social Affairs and Employment (SZW) – and implementing agencies such as the Tax and Customs Administration (Belastingdienst), the Benefits Service (Dienst Toeslagen), and the Education Executive Agency (DUO) offer a societally relevant context for collaboration on policy and implementation. More information about the SEO unit can be found here . What you bring We are looking for someone who combines scientific quality with connective leadership. You recognise yourself in the following profile: You lead your own research programme with demonstrable scientific and societal impact within social and/or economic psychology. You apply a connective leadership style that contributes to an inclusive, reflective, and psychologically safe work environment. You have a genuine commitment to Open Science, Recognition & Rewards, and Team Science. You have broad methodological expertise, a strong record of scientific publications, and a proven ability to secure external research funding. You have experience with academic leadership, governance tasks, and building sustainable national and international collaborations. You have leadership and governance experience, along with the ability to help shape strategic issues and act as a figurehead for overarching themes and partnerships. You have teaching experience at the bachelor's and master's level, and a track record of successfully supervising PhD candidates. You have excellent command of English, and (near) native proficiency in Dutch, or you are willing to attain professional fluency in Dutch within two years (funding for languages courses is provided). Leiden University expects professors to hold a University Teaching Qualification (BKO). If you do not yet hold this qualification, or an equivalent, you are willing to obtain it within two years. You live in the Netherlands and are officially registered as resident, or you are willing to relocate upon starting the position. If you immediately recognise yourself in this profile, or if do you not quite meet all the requirements, but you believe that this is the right job for you, we look forward to your application! What we offer Our goal is to work together to create a transparent and inclusive work environment in which everyone feels welcome and appreciated. Our organisation is always evolving and we need your ideas for improvement and innovation to take us further. We want to devote attention to your personal development. You can count on an enjoyable job within the socially relevant world of education and research. The University's challenging and international work environment is located just steps away from Leiden’s lively city centre or the bustling city centre of The Hague. We also want to work with you to devote attention to your health and vitality, for example with the fun activities we organise through Healthy University. We also offer: An employment contract for the duration of one year with the prospect of a permanent employment. This contract falls under the CLA of Dutch Universities; A salary between €7475 - €10.815 gross per month, based on a full-time appointment (38 hours) (Professor 2); A holiday allowance (8%), an end-of-year bonus (8,3%), and an attractive pension scheme at ABP; Full reimbursement of public transport commuting costs for home-to-work travel; Flexible working hours: as a standard, you are entitled to a minimum of 29 leave days on the basis of a full-time working week of 38 hours; you can also save for extra leave, for example by working 40 hours a week, and in this way accrue an extra 96 leave hours, or exchange 96 leave hours for a 36-hour week. Lots of options when it comes to secondary employment conditions; we can, for example, discuss options for a sabbatical or paid parental leave. Within our terms of employment individual choices model, you can exchange leave days and/or salary for benefits such as an advantageous sports subscription and bicycle scheme; If your work allows it, hybrid working is possible within the Netherlands; A home-working allowance (day and internet allowance) and attention for good workplaces. The University will also provide you with a laptop and a mobile telephone. For more information about employment conditions, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions What we value Promoting an inclusive community is central to Leiden University’s values and vision. We strive to be an open community in which all students and staff members feel valued and respected, and are able to fully develop themselves. We consider an inclusive academic environment, where students and staff can share their experiences and viewpoints, to be essential for the development of talent. Want to apply or find out more? If you want to apply straight away, click the application button. If you would like more information about what the job entails, please contact Wilco van Dijk, chair of the SEO-unit, at +31 6 43446432 or dijkwvan@fsw.leidenuniv.nl. You can apply until 23 October 2026; applications will be processed immediately. To help us get to know each other better, we follow a number of steps in the application procedure. For more information, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions We believe mobility is very important. That is why we are also publishing this vacancy internally. In case of equal suitability, we will give priority to the internal candidate. A pre-employment screening (references, diplomas, certificate of good conduct (VOG)) may be part of the selection procedure. Acquisition in response to this vacancy is not appreciated. If you nevertheless choose to send us CVs, no rights can be derived from this. #LI-Hybrid Apply now Information at a Glance Faculty / Unit: Faculty Social and Behavioural Sciences Contact with (Name + position): Wilco van Dijk, chair of the SEO-unit Contact Email address: +31 6 43446432 or dijkwvan@fsw.leidenuniv.nl Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Study programmes Bachelor's programmes Master's programmes PhD programmes Education for Professionals Summer Schools Other modes of study Bachelor's Open Days Master's Open Days Admission & Application Organisation Archaeology Governance and Global Affairs Humanities Law Medicine/LUMC Science Social and Behavioural Sciences African Studies Centre Leiden Honours Academy ICLON (Graduate School of Teaching) About us Working at Leiden University Support Leiden University Alumni Impact Leiden-Delft-Erasmus Universities Locations Disclaimer Cookies Privacy Contact × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data center \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary)  prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimizing video performance, or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like, and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies ​ ​"
    },
    "https://careers.universiteitleiden.nl/job/Postdoctoral-Position-Youth%2C-Digital-Media%2C-and-Democracy-in-Education-%281_0-FTE%2C-2-years%29/16932-en_US": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position: Youth, Digital Media, and Democracy in Education",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Position: Youth, Digital Media, and Democracy in Education (1.0 FTE, 2 years) Job Details | Universiteit Leiden We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies All jobs Change language English (United States) Nederlands (Nederland) My profile Internal vacancies Home Vacancies Job Description Postdoctoral Position: Youth, Digital Media, and Democracy in Education (1.0 FTE, 2 years) Job requisition Id: 16932 Job type: PhD positions Contract hours min: 38 Contract hours max: 38 Location: Leiden Applying is possible until: 10 November 2026 Apply now Postdoctoral Position: Youth, Digital Media, and Democracy in Education How do social media and digital technologies shape how adolescents understand societal issues and form democratic attitudes? How can education help them to build resillience to online influences? We are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to investigate these questions with us. Join our team at the Cognition in Development (CoDe) Lab led by Dr. Ili Ma at Leiden University. What you will do This postdoctoral project focusses on investigating educational interventions that aim to strengthen youths’ democratic values and resilience in the digital context. The postdoc will be embedded in a team which investigates how the digital information environments of adolescents relate to their beliefs and attitudes about societal issues, their democratic values and civic participation. We are looking for a candidate who is enthusiastic about the opportunity to do fundamental, impact-driven research together with adolescents, schools, and societal partners on this topic of high societal relevance. A background in developmental science, educational science, political psychology, social psychology, cognitive science, or a related field is desirable. Experience with the Dutch educational context, social media data, longitudinal data, or participatory youth research is an advantage though not a prerequisite. Your responsibilities will include: Designing and conducting longitudinal studies on educational interventions for adolescents and young adults Collaborating with youth, schools and other societal partners Publishing research findings in international scientific journals and impact reports for societal partners Presenting research at national and international conferences Co-supervising PhD student projects This is a fulltime (1.0 fte) 2-year position with a flexible starting date ranging from November 2026 to February 2027. This postdoctoral position is funded by the European Research Council, ERC starting grant DISINFORMED TEENS awarded to Dr. Ili Ma. The project offers the opportunity to conduct innovative and societally relevant research at the intersection of adolescent development, digital technology and democracy. You will become part of a supportive research community committed to working with youth and translating scientific insights into knowledge that can benefit schools, policy and society. Where you will work The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences consists of five institutes: the Centre for Science and Technology Studies, Cultural Anthropology and Development Sociology, Education and Child Studies, Political Science, and Psychology. The faculty is home to approximately 7,000 students and 1,000 staff members. Our institutes are dedicated not only to education but also to groundbreaking research that expands our understanding of human behavior and societal structures. What makes our faculty unique is the diversity of research topics, the variety of teaching approaches, and the structure of professional support. This provides you with the opportunity to explore and develop your interests and expertise. For an introduction to our faculty, visit our website: Welcome to the Leiden Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences – Leiden University . The Institute of Psychology trains new generations of students to address the challenges of our society, both now and in the future, through leading behavioral science research and education. This is achieved through collaboration, both within and outside the institute, in the field of psychology and related research areas, covering both fundamental and applied aspects. The broad bachelor’s program and a wide range of master’s specializations further contribute to this mission. Within the institute and the academic community, a dedicated and passionate atmosphere prevails. The themes of Health and Wellbeing, Social-Cognitive-Affective Decision Making, Development and Learning, and Advanced Behavioral Science Methods guide both research and education. The institute places great importance on fostering a safe and inclusive environment, with room for new developments such as open science, recognition and valorization, and interdisciplinarity, which are firmly embedded in the institute’s strategy. The institute, with approximately 5,000 students and 600 staff members, comprises the Institute Office, the Bachelor’s Education Department, and six research and teaching units: Health, Medical and Neuropsychology, Clinical Psychology, Cognitive Psychology, Developmental and Educational Psychology, Social and Organizational Psychology, and Methods and Statistics. What you bring We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher with: A PhD in developmental psychology, political psychology, computational social science, cognitive science or a related field, completed or expected to be completed before the position begins Experience in designing and conducting behavioural experiments Strong quantitative research skills, including experience with R and/or Python and preferably behavioural or computational modelling Experience with, or an interest in developing expertise in, donated social media data and other forms of digital trace data Strong analytical and organisational skills, including the ability to plan and conduct research independently Excellent written and spoken English, with the ability to communicate research to both academic and non-academic audiences Proficiency in Dutch is highly preferred, as the position involves regular interaction with adolescents, schools and societal partners Enthusiasm for working with adolescents, together with a careful and ethically sensitive approach to research involving youth and personal digital data A collaborative and inclusive mindset, openness to learning new methods and a willingness to share knowledge within and beyond academia Enthusiasm for translating scientific findings into practical applications and sharing them with schools, policymakers and other societal partners We recognise that strong candidates may bring different combinations of experience and expertise. We therefore encourage you to apply if the position fits your interests and you believe you could make a valuable contribution to the team. What we offer Our goal is to work together to create a transparent and inclusive work environment in which everyone feels welcome and appreciated. Our organisation is always evolving and we need your ideas for improvement and innovation to take us further. We want to devote attention to your personal development. You can count on an enjoyable job within the socially relevant world of education and research. The University's challenging and international work environment is located just steps away from Leiden’s lively city centre or the bustling city centre of The Hague. We also want to work with you to devote attention to your health and vitality, for example with the fun activities we organise through Healthy University. We also offer: An employment contract for (38 hours per week), initially for a period of 1 year, with the possibility of extension of 1 year after a positive evaluation. This contract falls under the CLA of Dutch Universities; A salary between €3706 - €5760 gross per month, based on a full-time appointment (38 hours) (Scale 10); A holiday allowance (8%), an end-of-year bonus (8,3%), and an attractive pension scheme at ABP; Full reimbursement of public transport commuting costs for home-to-work travel; Flexible working hours: as a standard, you are entitled to a minimum of 29 leave days on the basis of a full-time working week of 38 hours; you can also save for extra leave, for example by working 40 hours a week, and in this way accrue an extra 96 leave hours, or exchange 96 leave hours for a 36-hour week. Lots of options when it comes to secondary employment conditions; we can, for example, discuss options for a sabbatical or paid parental leave. Within our terms of employment individual choices model, you can exchange leave days and/or salary for benefits such as an advantageous sports subscription and bicycle scheme; If your work allows it, hybrid working is possible within the Netherlands; A home-working allowance (day and internet allowance) and attention for good workplaces. The University will also provide you with a laptop and a mobile telephone. For more information about employment conditions, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions What we value Promoting an inclusive community is central to Leiden University’s values and vision. We strive to be an open community in which all students and staff members feel valued and respected, and are able to fully develop themselves. We consider an inclusive academic environment, where students and staff can share their experiences and viewpoints, to be essential for the development of talent. Want to apply or find out more? If you want to apply straight away, click the application button. If you would like more information about what the job entails, please contact Dr. Ili Ma, at i.ma@fsw.leidenuniv.nl. You can apply until 10 November 2026; applications will be processed immediately. To help us get to know each other better, we follow a number of steps in the application procedure. For more information, see https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/working-at/job-application-procedure-and-employment-conditions We believe mobility is very important. That is why we are also publishing this vacancy internally. In case of equal suitability, we will give priority to the internal candidate. You live and are officially registered as resident in The Netherlands or are willing to relocate upon starting the position. A pre-employment screening (references, diplomas, certificate of good conduct (VOG)) may be part of the selection procedure. Acquisition in response to this vacancy is not appreciated. If you nevertheless choose to send us CVs, no rights can be derived from this. #LI-Hybrid Apply now Information at a Glance Faculty / Unit: Faculty Social and Behavioural Sciences Contact with (Name + position): Dr. Ili Ma Contact Email address: i.ma@fsw.leidenuniv.nl Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Study programmes Bachelor's programmes Master's programmes PhD programmes Education for Professionals Summer Schools Other modes of study Bachelor's Open Days Master's Open Days Admission & Application Organisation Archaeology Governance and Global Affairs Humanities Law Medicine/LUMC Science Social and Behavioural Sciences African Studies Centre Leiden Honours Academy ICLON (Graduate School of Teaching) About us Working at Leiden University Support Leiden University Alumni Impact Leiden-Delft-Erasmus Universities Locations Disclaimer Cookies Privacy Contact × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data center \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary)  prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimizing video performance, or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like, and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies ​ ​"
